    {
      "id": "bb914d31c08bb866160aba71774c8a7389c4f6a1",
      "individual_note": true,
      "resolvable": false,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 2617066284,
          "type": null,
          "body": "@jim.young as we discussed, add command-line options --profile (tshark -C) and --max-packets (tshark -c), which if set will be included in the tshark command line.  I think I prefer this to just having a catchall --extra-tshark-args\n\nI couldn't see an ideal way to detect a key press so that the script could report:\n- name of previous/next file being checked\n- progress through set of files being checked\n- summary of matches found so far?\n\nThe 'keyboard' module might be useful - it is in pip but may not be installed by default.  I thought there might be a way to make signals out of key presses and catch them (like currently do for signal.SIGINT), but it seems not.  We could overload SIGINT (Ctrl-C), by showing status/progress when first pressed, but if pressed again within a second or 2, actually exit.\n\nI did wonder about having an option to exit on the first matched file, but I would often see the match as it happens and decide whether I wanted to let it continue or not.  If this was done, would definitely want to set the exit code to reflect whether it found a match.",
          "author": {
            "id": 6810036,
            "username": "martin.r.mathieson",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Martin Mathieson",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
